   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c5971a86-cb39-4bd5-892e-b2decaf06e54",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Q1: Can we predict, using only information available at admission, whether a heart failure patient will be readmitted within 6 months — so hospitals can act preventively rather than reactively?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d0e30011-b14f-4ec4-a4f5-9c6d72af5cf0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#  Hypotheses\n",
    "**H₀ (null):** Admission-time clinical features (vitals, labs, comorbidity burden, HF type, demographics) have no predictive relationship with 6-month readmission — readmission is effectively random with respect to these variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**H₁ (alternate):** Admission-time clinical features do carry predictive signal for 6-month readmission — a model trained on them will perform meaningfully better than random guessing (AUC > 0.5)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0d87ae44-74ca-4e50-8392-191e8280ea20",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Parameters / Features Used"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3923f6b7-daf7-467a-ba33-f85ad35bd1ff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Objective:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fefd6b6d-9878-4e64-b799-22c0df13e8fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Objective is to predict the rate of readmission within 6 months at the time of admission itself**\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "75c07a5b-c4ee-42a1-b61a-31c63251e181",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Reasoning:\n",
    "Doctors want to know, right when a heart failure patient walks in, whether that patient is likely to come back to the hospital within the next 6 months — so they can step in early (extra follow-up, medication checks) instead of just waiting and reacting after it happens. We can only use information available at the moment of admission, since anything that happens after discharge wouldn't help the hospital plan ahead. Testing this with a prediction model tells us honestly how much the admission data alone can (or can't) forecast — which is genuinely useful either way."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e7f6478e-629b-4a79-af7a-5b13ba25e91a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Parameters and Their Clinical Connection to Readmission Risk\n",
    "\n",
    "| Parameter | Type | Connection to 6-month readmission risk |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `cci_score` | Numeric | More chronic conditions on board means more competing health problems that can independently trigger a return trip to the hospital, not just the heart itself. |\n",
    "| `systolic_blood_pressure` | Numeric | Abnormally low BP at admission can signal poor cardiac output; a key marker of hemodynamic instability. |\n",
    "| `diastolic_blood_pressure` | Numeric | High or low diastolic pressure reflects vascular tone/uncontrolled hypertension, a common driver of HF decompensation. |\n",
    "| `pulse` | Numeric | An elevated resting heart rate suggests the heart is compensating for reduced pumping efficiency — a sign of instability. |\n",
    "| `brain_natriuretic_peptide` | Numeric | Directly measures cardiac wall stress from fluid overload — the single most HF-specific lab in the dataset, and a well-established readmission predictor. |\n",
    "| `creatinine_enzymatic_method` | Numeric | Poor kidney function limits how aggressively diuretics can be used, tied to cardiorenal syndrome risk — a known driver of bounce-back admissions. |\n",
    "| `sodium` | Numeric | Low sodium (hyponatremia) reflects how far the body has fallen into fluid-retention compensation — already linked to worse outcomes in this analysis. |\n",
    "| `potassium` | Numeric | Electrolyte imbalance risk, especially relevant with diuretic/MRA use — abnormal levels can precipitate an event requiring readmission. |\n",
    "| `hemoglobin` | Numeric | Anemia increases cardiac workload (the heart pumps harder to deliver oxygen), independently worsening HF prognosis. |\n",
    "| `white_blood_cell` | Numeric | Can flag an underlying infection triggering the HF episode — a root cause that, if missed, could resurface after discharge. |\n",
    "| `platelet` | Numeric | General marker of physiological stress; included as a broad stability indicator. |\n",
    "| `lvef` | Numeric | Defines HF type and severity (HFrEF vs. HFpEF) — directly tied to which medications are indicated and how fragile pumping function is. |\n",
    "| `total_prescriptions_count` | Numeric | Proxy for medication complexity/polypharmacy — more medications can mean better guideline coverage, but also more risk of confusion managing them at home. |\n",
    "| `gender` | Categorical | Demographic context capturing baseline risk differences not reflected in labs alone. |\n",
    "| `bmi_category` | Categorical | Body-composition context; both very low and very high BMI carry distinct HF risk profiles. |\n",
    "| `admission_way` | Categorical | How urgently the patient arrived (Emergency vs. Non-Emergency) reflects how destabilized they already were before hospitalization. |\n",
    "| `type_of_heart_failure` | Categorical | Biventricular (both-sided) failure — the most common and most advanced form in this cohort — may carry distinct readmission risk from isolated left- or right-sided failure. |\n",
    "| `nyha_cardiac_function_classification` | Categorical | Captures how limited the patient's daily life already was *before* this admission — a direct measure of chronic disease severity. |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cc0c6b1a-d278-4429-8530-da470628b86e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
